Owner request: the CI host should not be written into a public repo.

The server now comes from WOODPECKER_SERVER in .env. Only the pipeline
COORDINATES are read off the commit status' target_url, and only its path is
matched — the scheme/host portion is discarded even when present, so a
target_url cannot reintroduce a hard-coded host. ALLOWED_WEBSITES.md records
the permission as "$WOODPECKER_SERVER (see .env)", documenting the policy
without publishing the host, and the CHANGELOG entry is scrubbed likewise.

HONEST LIMIT: this is forward-only. The hostname already landed in public
history via PR #94 (ALLOWED_WEBSITES.md + CHANGELOG.md), and removing it from
history would need a force-push, which is forbidden. This reduces further
exposure; it does not un-publish.

Two corrections folded in, both from measuring the live endpoint rather than
from the previous session's error text:

  - It is a 302 redirect to the Cloudflare Access login, NOT a 403. The
    effect is the same (blocked at the edge, Woodpecker never reached) but
    the code was wrong; some clients merely surface it as 403.
  - The fallback now tells the operator how to READ the failure, because
    the three codes have different causes and different fixes:
      302 -> not recognised as a service token at all (usually a truncated
             Client ID; the full value ends .access.<team>.cloudflareaccess.com
             and is ~70 chars, not ~39)
      403 -> token recognised but no policy with ACTION 'Service Auth'
             admits it — creating the token is not sufficient on its own
      401 -> past Access; the Woodpecker token is the problem
    That triage came from diagnosing a real failure against the live host.

Verified end to end on the merged tree: with all four vars set, the fetch is
genuinely ATTEMPTED (it reports "Woodpecker log fetch failed (HTTP 302);
falling back"), which proves the WOODPECKER_SERVER path, the coordinate
parse and the header assembly all work — the remaining 302 is the Client ID
value, not the code.

